the United Kingdom uses the pound  sterling and up until the 1940s the  pound was the dominant international  currency yet when you look at many  former British colonies you'll notice  that they use dollars and not pounds now  obviously we can't go over every former  British colony and so the two will  discuss a Canada and Australia because  these two nations maintained good  relations with Britain up until their  independence and both made conscious  efforts to drop the pound so why exactly  did these former British colonies use  dollars instead of pounds Canada dropped  the British pound in 1841 and replaced  it with its own the Canadian pound was  worth slightly less than the British one  and it wasn't long before it was dropped  for the brand new Canadian dollar the  reason why Canada opted to do this was  twofold and the first was a simple case  of proximity its nearest neighbor was a  major economy in Canada did a great deal  of trade with them which was only ever  really going to increase and for them it  was a simple case of neighbors having a  similar currency the second reason was  that since its inception the dollar had  always been a decimal currency what that  means is that it's simple since there's  only 100 cents to the dollar the British  Pound was not a decimal currency and  work like this each pound was made up of  20 shillings which in turn was made up  of 12 pennies and a penny was comprised  of four farthings thus a single pound  was made up of 960 farthings or 240  pennies and the value of these sub units  was very volatile now it should be noted  that despite Canada dropping the pound  it was still the world's primary  international currency after Britain  became the first major nation to  withdraw from the gold standard in 1931  it created what was called the sterling  area this was where all of Britain's  colonies peg their currency to the pound  at a fixed exchange rate and this was  followed by the Ottawa system also  called the Imperial preference system  which protects on other nations that  used a different currency in any trade  that wasn't carried out in pounds Canada  wasn't a member of the sterling area  because obviously but it did become a  part of the Imperial preference system  so what about Australia adopting the  dollar  it didn't neighbor the United States so  what reason would it have for dropping  the pound discussions about adopting a  new currency didn't really begin until  after the Second World War Britain was  largely bankrupted by this and so  Australia's main trading partner was  buying less someone who was buying more  though was America and given that  membership of the Sterling area made  selling to them harder a decision had to  be made  such in the 1950s Australian Prime  Minister Sir Robert Menzies ordered a  review into whether or not Australia  should continue to use the pound and the  answer was no the reason being that  Britain share of the global economy was  shrinking and so punishing the  Australian economy by preferring them  over the rest of the planet didn't make  much sense and one final reason was that  of identity Australia along with other  former British colonies wish to distance  themselves from the old imperial  Metropole in 1966 Australia ditched the  pound and adopted its own dollar with a  hundred cents and so that's why ex  British colonies dropped the pound for  the dollar the lack of decimalisation  made the pound more prone to  fluctuations which affected ordinary  people there was also the acceptance  that the United States was now the  world's dominant economy and that using  $1.00 would make trade with them much  smoother I hope you enjoyed this episode  and thank you for watching but thanks to  my patreon supporters whom you can see  on screen now and with an extra special  thanks to James Bissonnette Ike Danny  Maloney Colin Castleman Marvin Castle  Rob Waterhouse Aaron the white Jordan  long way Chris wicker Gustav Swan Gareth  Turner  Marcus